WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:09.003
- Hi, everyone, good afternoon.

00:00:10.644 --> 00:00:12.630
I'm Priya Natarajan,
I'm the current director

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:15.990
of the Franke Program in
Science and the Humanities.

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:18.087
I'm a astrophysicist.

00:00:18.087 --> 00:00:20.850
I am just really delighted you're here.

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:23.730
This is to really give you a warm welcome

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:26.970
to our first convening this fall

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:28.530
as we open up our programming

00:00:28.530 --> 00:00:31.080
for this academic year in person,

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:35.250
and I'm really grateful
that you all are here.

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:38.280
We have a very exciting
event for this evening,

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:40.740
a wonderful, thought-provoking film

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:43.140
by a very young talented filmmaker,

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:46.110
and he happens to be a Yale alum as well.

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:48.300
More about that in just a minute.

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:52.380
First, I would like to start
with thanking our benefactors,

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:54.720
Mr. and Mrs. Richard and Barbara Franke,

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:57.090
for their generosity in supporting us

00:00:57.090 --> 00:00:59.880
in multiple other activities at Yale.

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:04.110
As you all know, we lost
Mr. Franke this summer,

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:06.750
and so there will be a memorial service

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:10.260
to honor his legacy, his contributions,

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:12.030
and his unflagging support

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:15.150
for many cross-disciplinary
activities at Yale.

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:18.990
As a flagship interdisciplinary
program at Yale

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:21.180
that bridges the so-called
sort of, you know,

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:25.230
two cultures that C.P. Snow
came up with in the 1950s,

00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:27.540
the sciences and the humanities,

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:30.720
here, we have been catalyzing
intellectual collaborations

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:33.120
across disciplinary boundaries and silos,

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:35.760
and as every year, we are now delighted

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:38.520
to welcome many of our
young undergraduate,

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:41.340
graduate, and post-doctoral Franke Fellows

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:46.200
whose work actually
crosses many disciplines.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:48.090
And before we move on to the event,

00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:50.400
I'm required to give you this notice.

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:52.680
This event is being
recorded and photographed

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:55.590
for educational, archival,
and promotional purposes,

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:58.500
including use in print and on the internet

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:00.030
and other forms of media.

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:01.740
By attending this event today,

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:04.620
you agree to the possibility
of your voice or likeness

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:07.950
captured by these means
and used for such purposes

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:09.960
without compensation to you

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:14.400
and hereby waive any related
right of privacy or publicity.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:16.770
So moving on to today's program,

00:02:16.770 --> 00:02:19.110
we are screening the film "Unconformity"

00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:21.480
by the filmmaker Jonathan DiMaio,

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:24.063
after which we will open
up the floor to a Q&amp;A,

00:02:25.782 --> 00:02:29.190
and we are really fortunate
to have him here this evening.

00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:33.690
Jonathan, welcome back to Yale, back home.

00:02:33.690 --> 00:02:36.180
After graduating in 2009,

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:39.150
Jon received Yale's
Parker Huang Fellowship,

00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:42.870
which he used to help found Yspaniola,

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:47.687
a nonprofit that began as
a Yale undergraduate group.

00:02:47.687 --> 00:02:50.100
Yspaniola is an educational organization

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:52.800
that works with Dominicans
of Haitian descent

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:54.690
who are at risk of statelessness,

00:02:54.690 --> 00:02:56.370
and the organization still runs

00:02:56.370 --> 00:02:58.350
a university scholarship program,

00:02:58.350 --> 00:03:00.660
which Jon started in 2010.

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:05.660
And he spent the early 2010s
between Boston, New York,

00:03:06.030 --> 00:03:08.970
and the Dominican Republic
working on film sets,

00:03:08.970 --> 00:03:12.930
making his own short films,
and setting up programs

00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:15.750
and seeking funding for his nonprofit.

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:19.170
His 2014 short film
"Without the Ancestors"

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:20.730
played at numerous festivals

00:03:20.730 --> 00:03:22.110
in the United States and Europe,

00:03:22.110 --> 00:03:24.510
including the Pan African Film Festival

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:27.810
and the Zinebi in Bilbao,
and his music videos

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:30.420
with Dominican rappers Chokola Y Gimby

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:34.200
helped launch the duo to
national prominence in DR.

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:37.680
At the beginning of 2014, Jon
travel to the Mojave Desert

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:40.530
to be a field camp manager.

00:03:40.530 --> 00:03:43.860
Talk about the versatility
in the talented,

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:45.180
you know, Yale undergraduate.

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:48.300
It's just a range of, I mean,
I've been here for 20 years,

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:50.550
and I'm still completely astounded

00:03:50.550 --> 00:03:55.410
at the multifaceted talents
these young people have.

00:03:55.410 --> 00:03:58.260
So he became a camp
manager, field camp manager

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:02.610
for a geology class run by
a Yale college classmate.

00:04:02.610 --> 00:04:05.190
This trip sparked an
interest in land rights

00:04:05.190 --> 00:04:07.020
and geology in the West.

00:04:07.020 --> 00:04:09.570
Jon started traveling West
to meet with geologists,

00:04:09.570 --> 00:04:12.480
ranchers, Native American
tribes, and archivists.

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:16.020
And after years of research,
writing, and outreach,

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:18.150
his work has led to this remarkable

00:04:18.150 --> 00:04:20.310
feature film, "Unconformity."

00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:22.590
Along with "Unconformity,"
Jon is currently

00:04:22.590 --> 00:04:25.050
in post-production on two short films.

00:04:25.050 --> 00:04:26.640
Hopefully, Jonathan, you will tell us

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:29.100
a little bit more about it before the Q&amp;A,

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:33.540
dying to hear more, and
developing many other movies.

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:37.050
And I think, you know, the
testimony to his talent

00:04:37.050 --> 00:04:39.270
is that not only was he
just the field camp manager,

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:42.660
but he used that experience
to learn and teach us all

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:44.700
something about the complexities

00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:48.840
of what is going on in the West.

00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:50.640
So before starting today's event

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:53.580
and we start with the
screening of the film,

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:57.210
I would like to mention a
couple of upcoming events.

00:04:57.210 --> 00:05:00.090
So we have an ongoing
Learning by Doing project

00:05:00.090 --> 00:05:02.370
for which we are soliciting videos.

00:05:02.370 --> 00:05:05.160
These are small science
do-it-at-home experimental videos

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:07.860
that you can send us
that we share with kids

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:09.360
around the world.

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:12.330
And as part of our Understanding
the Nature of Inference

00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:15.930
colloquium series, it's
a virtual talk series,

00:05:15.930 --> 00:05:17.640
Professor Alexander Paseau,

00:05:17.640 --> 00:05:19.800
a distinguished professor
of philosophy at Oxford,

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:23.700
will be giving the talk on
September 28th at 3:00 PM.

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:25.680
And then we have lined up for you

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:29.100
a virtual talk by journalist Esther Honig

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:32.220
on the sad state of
agricultural degradation

00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:36.270
in a small Mexican fishing
village in early November.

00:05:36.270 --> 00:05:39.240
And later on in November,
we have a very exciting talk

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:43.110
by another of our
illustrious young Yale alums,

00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:45.420
the star architect James Ramsey,

00:05:45.420 --> 00:05:47.670
who is a former NASA engineer as well,

00:05:47.670 --> 00:05:49.530
and he'll be talking
to us on November 10th.

00:05:49.530 --> 00:05:51.963
So many treats to come.

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:57.570
And without further ado,
let me just turn over

00:05:57.570 --> 00:06:00.090
to Jonathan's film "Unconformity."

00:06:00.090 --> 00:06:02.250
And thank you, Jonathan, for being here,

00:06:02.250 --> 00:06:04.710
and we're really looking
forward to talking to you

00:06:04.710 --> 00:06:06.360
after the screening.

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:08.190
And thanks everyone, once again.

00:06:08.190 --> 00:06:09.753
Let's enjoy this film.

00:06:12.148 --> 00:06:15.398
(audience applauding)

00:06:31.740 --> 00:06:32.973
Very, very moving.

00:06:35.580 --> 00:06:37.050
I sort of feel very emotional. (laughs)

00:06:37.050 --> 00:06:41.223
There were many aspects about, you know,

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:46.860
academic life, competitiveness,
and our sort of obsession

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:50.520
with the materialistic/consumerist
catalyst culture.

00:06:50.520 --> 00:06:54.000
There's just so many things
that we could talk about.

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:56.100
I just want to jumpstart the conversation

00:06:56.100 --> 00:06:59.013
and then open it up to everyone else.

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:03.960
So what about your field trip camp,

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:07.470
what really got you
started on sort of looking

00:07:07.470 --> 00:07:10.950
at this sort of intersection
of really complex

00:07:10.950 --> 00:07:15.783
but really moving, upsetting issues?

00:07:17.220 --> 00:07:19.830
- So I was in the desert for a month.

00:07:19.830 --> 00:07:21.870
Mostly during the day, I was by myself,

00:07:21.870 --> 00:07:24.510
because the geologists
would go out to the field,

00:07:24.510 --> 00:07:27.150
and I was actually editing
the film at the time.

00:07:27.150 --> 00:07:29.880
So I would edit the film and hang out

00:07:29.880 --> 00:07:32.610
with everything else in the desert.

00:07:32.610 --> 00:07:34.620
And there were lots of
strange things happening,

00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:36.840
'cause we were near Twentynine Palms.

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:40.230
You would run across people, ranchers,

00:07:40.230 --> 00:07:41.780
other people out in the desert.

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:46.080
And it was right around
the time that Cliven Bundy

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:51.080
and his family violently
overtook some federal property,

00:07:51.450 --> 00:07:54.090
or they weren't paying
their ranching fees.

00:07:54.090 --> 00:07:58.260
So I became interested in that and about,

00:07:58.260 --> 00:07:59.340
wanted to learn about ranching,

00:07:59.340 --> 00:08:00.900
and I went out and met with ranchers

00:08:00.900 --> 00:08:02.700
and started hearing their stories.

00:08:02.700 --> 00:08:05.223
And the Bundys are obviously extremists,

00:08:07.267 --> 00:08:09.210
and the initial drafts of the film

00:08:09.210 --> 00:08:12.630
were more about Bundy-style people

00:08:12.630 --> 00:08:14.970
with those types of views, but it wasn't,

00:08:14.970 --> 00:08:16.950
it ended up where I wanted
to go with the film,

00:08:16.950 --> 00:08:20.460
because most ranchers aren't Bundys.

00:08:20.460 --> 00:08:25.460
- Right, and I guess somewhere in there,

00:08:26.190 --> 00:08:28.713
obviously academic culture fascinates you,

00:08:30.320 --> 00:08:34.490
and so I was curious about
that character of Alex.

00:08:36.750 --> 00:08:40.560
And so what sort of, was
there a particular person

00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:42.600
that inspired you there, or is this,

00:08:42.600 --> 00:08:46.080
this is like an archetype that
you kind of pulled out of?

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:48.060
- Yeah, more of an archetype,
more of an archetype,

00:08:48.060 --> 00:08:49.653
not one particular person.

00:08:51.090 --> 00:08:54.750
And I just, you know, she's very driven,

00:08:54.750 --> 00:08:56.670
and she's very focused,
and she's very selfish

00:08:56.670 --> 00:08:58.230
in a lot of ways throughout the film,

00:08:58.230 --> 00:09:01.413
and it's about her journey
of learning not to be.

00:09:02.970 --> 00:09:05.370
But there wasn't a particular
person that inspired it.

00:09:05.370 --> 00:09:08.670
But a lot of the academic culture was,

00:09:08.670 --> 00:09:11.010
and that sort of competitive nature,

00:09:11.010 --> 00:09:12.720
I had a lot of friends
who were in academia

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:16.890
after we all graduated
and hearing their stories

00:09:16.890 --> 00:09:19.560
and then honestly my
experiences in the film world

00:09:19.560 --> 00:09:22.890
and seeing how people behaved
and trying to get credit.

00:09:22.890 --> 00:09:25.650
And so it was a combination
of those things.

00:09:25.650 --> 00:09:28.290
- Yeah, well, I thought it
was just beautifully done.

00:09:28.290 --> 00:09:32.220
And anyway, I would like
to open up to others

00:09:32.220 --> 00:09:33.840
and not hog Jonathan completely.

00:09:33.840 --> 00:09:36.240
So if anyone else has questions,

00:09:36.240 --> 00:09:37.860
I think there is a roving microphone.

00:09:37.860 --> 00:09:39.453
Or did you wanna take this one?

00:09:40.470 --> 00:09:42.660
How do we wanna do this?

00:09:42.660 --> 00:09:44.340
- [Staff Member] There were
another microphone earlier.

00:09:44.340 --> 00:09:45.173
I don't know.

00:09:45.173 --> 00:09:49.590
- Can you hear me okay if I speak back?

00:09:49.590 --> 00:09:51.450
- Yeah, we can lend our microphones.

00:09:51.450 --> 00:09:55.260
I think we're both, thank you.

00:09:55.260 --> 00:09:56.970
- [Staff Member] I think just have people-

00:09:56.970 --> 00:09:59.061
- Stand up and speak, okay.

00:09:59.061 --> 00:09:59.970
- [Staff Member] Because-

00:09:59.970 --> 00:10:01.950
- We may not be able
to record this, right?

00:10:01.950 --> 00:10:03.480
Or are you recording?
- Oh yeah, no.

00:10:03.480 --> 00:10:04.920
Let me just spin this around.

00:10:04.920 --> 00:10:05.753
- Oh, okay.
- If you wanna

00:10:05.753 --> 00:10:10.611
just have people ask a
question, I'll pick it up.

00:10:10.611 --> 00:10:11.444
- Okay.
- Okay.

00:10:12.600 --> 00:10:14.650
And we can hear them in the room anyways.

00:10:16.590 --> 00:10:18.766
Anyone has a burning question?

00:10:18.766 --> 00:10:20.089
(audience member speaks faintly)

00:10:20.089 --> 00:10:23.220
(audience chuckles)

00:10:23.220 --> 00:10:24.053
- Yes.

00:10:25.020 --> 00:10:27.413
- [Audience Member] I'm interested
in the composition of it.

00:10:28.860 --> 00:10:31.770
And I guess it would be useful

00:10:31.770 --> 00:10:35.883
to hear you say more about the process,

00:10:37.764 --> 00:10:40.083
'cause it's a very, very visual film.

00:10:42.390 --> 00:10:44.090
You know, like the shots are very-

00:10:44.943 --> 00:10:46.816
- [Staff Member] Just talk in there.

00:10:46.816 --> 00:10:49.649
(audience laughs)

00:10:52.320 --> 00:10:54.030
- Sorry, I'm getting used to this,

00:10:56.217 --> 00:10:57.963
and I'm glad it's being recorded.

00:10:59.850 --> 00:11:02.133
So I'm thinking about the, I guess the,

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:06.070
your own interest in narrative

00:11:07.159 --> 00:11:11.310
and then also image
because it's, you know,

00:11:11.310 --> 00:11:13.713
it's very, like I said,
it's a very visual film,

00:11:14.770 --> 00:11:16.920
you know, especially the beginning stages

00:11:16.920 --> 00:11:18.840
when she's alone and the solitude,

00:11:18.840 --> 00:11:23.840
and there's kind of
transcendental insistence,

00:11:25.616 --> 00:11:27.390
it's a kind of Edward Hall

00:11:27.390 --> 00:11:29.610
and Anna Abbey kind of feel to it.

00:11:29.610 --> 00:11:32.700
So I'm interested in hearing you talk

00:11:32.700 --> 00:11:35.500
about where in your composing

00:11:39.092 --> 00:11:42.993
the images and your
construction of images comes in.

00:11:45.450 --> 00:11:49.680
Does that come before
parts of the narrative?

00:11:49.680 --> 00:11:52.080
Does it arise out of the narrative.

00:11:52.080 --> 00:11:54.690
Do you think, "Okay, I've
worked through the narrative,

00:11:54.690 --> 00:11:59.100
and then when we've settled
on a location and all that,

00:11:59.100 --> 00:12:01.200
then I'll figure out the images"?

00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:02.670
I mean, it doesn't have to be either/or,

00:12:02.670 --> 00:12:04.727
but it would be interesting to hear

00:12:04.727 --> 00:12:09.727
like the sort of procedural
rhythm, compositional rhythm.

00:12:10.020 --> 00:12:11.220
Does that make sense?

00:12:11.220 --> 00:12:15.960
- Yeah, so a lot of it
is you find the location

00:12:15.960 --> 00:12:18.210
and then you pick the best shot.

00:12:18.210 --> 00:12:19.830
A fair amount of it comes from that.

00:12:19.830 --> 00:12:24.273
I knew that I wanted a lot
of wides, medium wides,

00:12:25.200 --> 00:12:27.390
because I wanted to show
the characters in the space,

00:12:27.390 --> 00:12:32.390
and I wanted people to
feel that, so, you know.

00:12:34.080 --> 00:12:37.650
When I met with Hana, our
director of photography,

00:12:37.650 --> 00:12:39.300
we basically sat down

00:12:39.300 --> 00:12:41.070
and planned every single shot in the film.

00:12:41.070 --> 00:12:44.310
And what happens often is
you have to cut on the day,

00:12:44.310 --> 00:12:45.750
you have to cut a third of them

00:12:45.750 --> 00:12:47.850
because you don't have time, but a lot of,

00:12:47.850 --> 00:12:50.730
you plan out sort of where you want to,

00:12:50.730 --> 00:12:55.290
where the camera goes,
and you develop a style.

00:12:55.290 --> 00:12:56.850
You look at reference shots.

00:12:56.850 --> 00:12:59.550
I was looking at a lot of photography.

00:12:59.550 --> 00:13:02.050
I was looking at a lot of
Robert Adams photography

00:13:03.270 --> 00:13:04.290
when we were in pre-production

00:13:04.290 --> 00:13:08.580
and showing those images to
her and talking about them.

00:13:08.580 --> 00:13:11.490
And then also, you know, some
of the footage that we got

00:13:11.490 --> 00:13:14.280
I wasn't even there for,
because we had B-roll.

00:13:14.280 --> 00:13:15.870
We had a friend, I had a friend come out,

00:13:15.870 --> 00:13:17.970
and we basically talked
about the aesthetic style.

00:13:17.970 --> 00:13:21.150
And he came out with his
drone and his camera,

00:13:21.150 --> 00:13:23.760
and he went out and spent a couple days

00:13:23.760 --> 00:13:25.713
just finding footage.

00:13:26.910 --> 00:13:30.663
So it's sort of a
combination of those things.

00:13:32.010 --> 00:13:35.010
- Yeah, so actually I did
wanna follow up and ask,

00:13:35.010 --> 00:13:39.900
so was there a lot of
drone used for this, or?

00:13:39.900 --> 00:13:41.370
- Yeah, and some of
them were pretty obvious

00:13:41.370 --> 00:13:43.200
where you see the camera
following or flying

00:13:43.200 --> 00:13:44.580
or very high in the sky.

00:13:44.580 --> 00:13:47.970
But some of it is, you
know, when you have a camera

00:13:47.970 --> 00:13:49.830
on the ground in the
desert, you don't really get

00:13:49.830 --> 00:13:51.480
that perspective and that deep view.

00:13:51.480 --> 00:13:53.010
And so there are a bunch of still shots

00:13:53.010 --> 00:13:54.540
where we just had the camera go up

00:13:54.540 --> 00:13:57.120
and look straight out
because it gives you,

00:13:57.120 --> 00:13:59.550
it just allows you to see the
contours of the desert better.

00:13:59.550 --> 00:14:00.990
And you wouldn't know
that it's a drone shot

00:14:00.990 --> 00:14:02.370
because usually you think drone,

00:14:02.370 --> 00:14:05.250
you think fancy helicopter-style shots.

00:14:05.250 --> 00:14:07.680
And my favorite drone shot is the one

00:14:07.680 --> 00:14:11.820
when they're on the ATV,
and the camera just,

00:14:11.820 --> 00:14:16.820
it jibs down to the fence,
because in traditional Hollywood,

00:14:17.520 --> 00:14:20.190
that shot would've cost by itself

00:14:20.190 --> 00:14:22.260
the level of the entire production budget.

00:14:22.260 --> 00:14:25.080
But with a drone, you just
have a guy who's talented,

00:14:25.080 --> 00:14:27.540
and he does the shot, and it just opens up

00:14:27.540 --> 00:14:28.890
what you're able to do, it's really,

00:14:28.890 --> 00:14:30.900
and it reads as a jib shot,

00:14:30.900 --> 00:14:33.090
which is a technical fun shot.

00:14:33.090 --> 00:14:34.680
- I mean, you know, as an astronomer,

00:14:34.680 --> 00:14:36.180
I can't resist asking, right?

00:14:36.180 --> 00:14:38.130
How come you didn't show the night sky?

00:14:39.425 --> 00:14:40.719
- (laughs) 'Cause the camera-
- The stars, they are,

00:14:40.719 --> 00:14:41.552
they're so beautiful.

00:14:41.552 --> 00:14:42.897
- I know, the camera, so-
- And they're together.

00:14:42.897 --> 00:14:44.310
- I'd just read an article

00:14:44.310 --> 00:14:47.550
about this new film by
Jordan Peele, "Nope,"

00:14:47.550 --> 00:14:51.870
and he used a new photography
method to shoot the sky

00:14:51.870 --> 00:14:54.810
that involves infrared
cameras and film cameras

00:14:54.810 --> 00:14:55.830
and then superimposing them.

00:14:55.830 --> 00:14:58.140
So if we had known about that

00:14:58.140 --> 00:14:59.381
four years ago-
- You would've done it.

00:14:59.381 --> 00:15:01.881
- We would've done it, but
it wasn't in the cards.

00:15:02.940 --> 00:15:04.650
- Well, I mean, I
realize it's about rocks.

00:15:04.650 --> 00:15:06.210
I mean, it's not about star space.

00:15:06.210 --> 00:15:07.740
- Yeah. (laughs)
- Yeah.

00:15:07.740 --> 00:15:08.573
- Vic.

00:15:08.573 --> 00:15:09.870
- [Vic] By the way, that's
what the Webb telescope

00:15:09.870 --> 00:15:13.796
does now, combines infrared
and regular photography.

00:15:13.796 --> 00:15:16.890
I just, well, I just wanted
to say, I'm not a film expert.

00:15:16.890 --> 00:15:20.100
I've known Jon for a long
time, and in my freshman year

00:15:20.100 --> 00:15:22.920
in 1970 at the Law School auditorium,

00:15:22.920 --> 00:15:26.460
I went to see "All About Eve,"
and Joseph Mankiewicz himself

00:15:26.460 --> 00:15:28.110
was there to answer questions,

00:15:28.110 --> 00:15:30.701
and I feel like I'm
continuum for the same thing.

00:15:30.701 --> 00:15:31.980
So congratulations.
(audience laughs)

00:15:31.980 --> 00:15:32.813
- Thank you.

00:15:37.590 --> 00:15:40.603
- Who wants the catch
the box next? (laughs)

00:15:44.370 --> 00:15:46.350
- [Audience Member] Jonathan,
it was wonderful to see it

00:15:46.350 --> 00:15:50.070
on the big screen after having
seen it on the computer.

00:15:50.070 --> 00:15:54.180
and I realized that there
was a lot that I had missed

00:15:54.180 --> 00:15:57.963
the first time and a
lot that became clearer.

00:16:01.410 --> 00:16:05.400
I was interested in how
you combined the story,

00:16:05.400 --> 00:16:08.340
the personality of the young woman,

00:16:08.340 --> 00:16:13.050
the main character and the landscape.

00:16:13.050 --> 00:16:14.083
What came first to you?

00:16:14.083 --> 00:16:18.390
Was it the desolateness of the
desert, or what was it that,

00:16:18.390 --> 00:16:21.870
because they all complement
each other obviously,

00:16:21.870 --> 00:16:24.420
but what came first when
you were thinking about it?

00:16:24.420 --> 00:16:26.870
Or how did you get through
that creative process?

00:16:29.988 --> 00:16:31.470
- It's hard to say.

00:16:31.470 --> 00:16:34.470
I think part of it was, you know,

00:16:34.470 --> 00:16:36.030
I went out to the desert with a friend

00:16:36.030 --> 00:16:37.710
who I met the first week at Yale,

00:16:37.710 --> 00:16:39.840
and seeing her go out into the desert

00:16:39.840 --> 00:16:41.730
and then meeting more geologists,

00:16:41.730 --> 00:16:45.300
who they just go out sometimes
for a year by themselves

00:16:45.300 --> 00:16:47.673
and live in Death Valley
just doing research.

00:16:48.960 --> 00:16:52.050
And of course my experience
out there was formative

00:16:52.050 --> 00:16:56.880
to being in that space,
and I became interested

00:16:56.880 --> 00:17:00.450
in who does that, who goes out there

00:17:00.450 --> 00:17:02.523
and spends time alone like that,

00:17:03.750 --> 00:17:05.913
because it's not what most people do.

00:17:11.370 --> 00:17:14.673
- So I'm just curious,
from your point of view,

00:17:15.690 --> 00:17:18.783
what do you expect the
viewer to take away?

00:17:19.650 --> 00:17:21.600
Do you, I mean, do you
have an expectation?

00:17:21.600 --> 00:17:23.133
I mean, you must, right?

00:17:27.060 --> 00:17:28.410
- I don't know, I try to,

00:17:28.410 --> 00:17:31.530
I don't want people to have a
specific reaction to the film.

00:17:31.530 --> 00:17:34.443
I want them to enjoy it for
the reasons they enjoy it.

00:17:35.610 --> 00:17:37.521
It's a hard question to
ask a filmmaker. (laughs)

00:17:37.521 --> 00:17:38.354
- Right.

00:17:38.354 --> 00:17:39.741
- Sorry, I don't, yeah.
- (laughs) I know that.

00:17:39.741 --> 00:17:40.574
(object clangs)

00:17:40.574 --> 00:17:45.574
Oops, but now that you've
seen it all come together,

00:17:46.350 --> 00:17:49.560
I mean, was there a portion
of the film, the shooting,

00:17:49.560 --> 00:17:52.380
the actual writing, as well as the filming

00:17:52.380 --> 00:17:54.480
that was particularly challenging for you?

00:17:57.090 --> 00:17:59.160
- I mean, filming in the desert

00:17:59.160 --> 00:18:01.503
on a low budget is very hard.

00:18:02.604 --> 00:18:05.070
And the biggest logistical
challenge really

00:18:05.070 --> 00:18:07.860
was just the distances that, you know,

00:18:07.860 --> 00:18:10.830
we were near a small town,
but we were 20 minutes away,

00:18:10.830 --> 00:18:13.560
and we were on two different ranches.

00:18:13.560 --> 00:18:15.420
You know, we were moving
around the ranches

00:18:15.420 --> 00:18:18.720
with a crew and location
and just making sure

00:18:18.720 --> 00:18:21.900
everything is in the right
place at the right time is hard

00:18:21.900 --> 00:18:24.690
when sometimes you
don't have phone service

00:18:24.690 --> 00:18:26.670
or you don't have walkie-talkies.

00:18:26.670 --> 00:18:28.413
So that that's a big challenge.

00:18:31.144 --> 00:18:36.030
And post-production, you
know, at first I hated it

00:18:36.030 --> 00:18:38.340
because it's really painful
to sit with your footage

00:18:38.340 --> 00:18:40.007
and try to figure out how
to make a movie out of it.

00:18:40.007 --> 00:18:41.700
- Right.
- But then it becomes

00:18:41.700 --> 00:18:43.890
this really kind of amazing process

00:18:43.890 --> 00:18:46.110
where you just sort of
layer in these elements,

00:18:46.110 --> 00:18:48.000
and you get to see the film become

00:18:48.000 --> 00:18:50.700
what it is going to become,
and it's really exciting.

00:18:50.700 --> 00:18:55.140
So that is challenging
because it takes so much time.

00:18:55.140 --> 00:18:58.290
We spent 18 days in principal photography,

00:18:58.290 --> 00:19:01.080
a couple days of reshoots,
had to go back to Vegas

00:19:01.080 --> 00:19:03.183
to get some B-roll during the pandemic.

00:19:04.350 --> 00:19:07.740
But really you're only
filming for 25 days,

00:19:07.740 --> 00:19:10.950
and then you spend two
years in an editing room.

00:19:10.950 --> 00:19:11.910
- Wow.
- Getting feedback,

00:19:11.910 --> 00:19:14.220
doing test screenings, going to festivals,

00:19:14.220 --> 00:19:17.493
and doing the music
itself took over a year.

00:19:18.693 --> 00:19:19.890
- [Priya] Yeah, it's hauntingly beautiful.

00:19:19.890 --> 00:19:21.270
- Yeah, Conor's a-
- It's really, yeah.

00:19:21.270 --> 00:19:22.803
- He's an amazing composer.

00:19:24.300 --> 00:19:26.430
Yeah, it was a super, that was
an incredible process, too,

00:19:26.430 --> 00:19:29.613
because he was, we would
get on a, you know,

00:19:29.613 --> 00:19:30.570
we would get on a call and just talk

00:19:30.570 --> 00:19:33.690
about what instrumentation
to use, what to do.

00:19:33.690 --> 00:19:36.810
And we were on a call together
when the Capitol was stormed.

00:19:36.810 --> 00:19:38.670
So every January 6th now,

00:19:38.670 --> 00:19:42.180
Conor and I have a reunion
call to commemorate that,

00:19:42.180 --> 00:19:43.320
because we just stopped working

00:19:43.320 --> 00:19:45.063
and watched the news together, so.

00:19:48.180 --> 00:19:49.560
- [Priya] Any other questions?

00:19:49.560 --> 00:19:51.480
There's a question in the back.

00:19:51.480 --> 00:19:54.180
- [Audience Member] How
did you find the actors?

00:19:54.180 --> 00:19:55.680
- We worked at a casting director,

00:19:55.680 --> 00:19:58.320
so through one of our producers,

00:19:58.320 --> 00:20:02.700
put us in touch with casting
a director who was quite good.

00:20:02.700 --> 00:20:04.650
And through sort of a series of events,

00:20:04.650 --> 00:20:05.730
we weren't going to work with her,

00:20:05.730 --> 00:20:08.460
but then she ended up
being on the project,

00:20:08.460 --> 00:20:09.870
and it just opened doors for us

00:20:09.870 --> 00:20:11.910
in terms of getting people that were good,

00:20:11.910 --> 00:20:14.670
because she was very well respected.

00:20:14.670 --> 00:20:18.600
And so, you know, it's
a very low-budget film.

00:20:18.600 --> 00:20:21.480
And so agents obviously are
asking for more and more money,

00:20:21.480 --> 00:20:23.227
but she was basically able to say,

00:20:23.227 --> 00:20:24.630
"Look, this is the project.

00:20:24.630 --> 00:20:25.770
This is how much money they have.

00:20:25.770 --> 00:20:27.203
Do you wanna be in it?"

00:20:27.203 --> 00:20:30.813
And really helped that process.

00:20:37.740 --> 00:20:40.290
- I guess next-
- The casting was wonderful.

00:20:40.290 --> 00:20:41.123
- Thank you.

00:20:41.123 --> 00:20:41.956
- [Audience Member]
Everybody was extremely

00:20:41.956 --> 00:20:44.190
believable in their part.

00:20:44.190 --> 00:20:46.740
I was very taken by the father.

00:20:46.740 --> 00:20:51.720
He could've been a one-dimensional
kind of rancher type,

00:20:51.720 --> 00:20:55.320
but you brought out many
different parts of him,

00:20:55.320 --> 00:20:57.420
and he had a very expressive face.

00:20:57.420 --> 00:20:58.470
- Yes.
- Yeah, he's wonderful.

00:20:58.470 --> 00:20:59.400
- Yeah.
- Yeah, he was,

00:20:59.400 --> 00:21:01.140
Jeremy's a fantastic actor.

00:21:01.140 --> 00:21:06.090
He often plays security
guard types on TV shows.

00:21:06.090 --> 00:21:09.300
People recognize him, but
he's got an incredible range.

00:21:09.300 --> 00:21:12.210
And he is also very fun to have on set.

00:21:12.210 --> 00:21:15.240
The first week, we were only
working with Jack and Alex,

00:21:15.240 --> 00:21:16.620
the two young actors, and you know,

00:21:16.620 --> 00:21:18.570
that were earlier movies for them.

00:21:18.570 --> 00:21:19.530
They haven't done very much,

00:21:19.530 --> 00:21:21.500
and everyone was kind of nervous.

00:21:21.500 --> 00:21:23.370
And Jeremy came the second week

00:21:23.370 --> 00:21:25.260
and was just like rolling
around in the dirt

00:21:25.260 --> 00:21:28.200
and making dad jokes, and
everyone kind of loosened up.

00:21:28.200 --> 00:21:30.360
And so it was really, he was a lot of fun

00:21:30.360 --> 00:21:32.523
to have around, and he was great.

00:21:34.860 --> 00:21:35.693
- [Audience Member] Jonathan,

00:21:35.693 --> 00:21:37.590
it's also my second time watching this,

00:21:37.590 --> 00:21:39.903
and I noticed a lot of things this time.

00:21:41.460 --> 00:21:43.800
I wanna really congratulate you

00:21:43.800 --> 00:21:47.670
on all the breathing
space you gave this movie,

00:21:47.670 --> 00:21:51.360
visually and in the
dialogue and in the music.

00:21:51.360 --> 00:21:54.570
There's just a lot of, not silence,

00:21:54.570 --> 00:21:58.380
but slow down or spaces.

00:21:58.380 --> 00:22:03.380
It just, and they spoke a lot to the plot.

00:22:03.660 --> 00:22:08.010
And that was my second,
actually more my question

00:22:08.010 --> 00:22:10.860
was how you came up
with such a great plot.

00:22:10.860 --> 00:22:15.860
Was this something that
kind of came to you at once,

00:22:15.960 --> 00:22:20.310
or did it develop after
you started a little bit?

00:22:20.310 --> 00:22:22.230
It's a fairly intricate plot,

00:22:22.230 --> 00:22:27.120
but it's very, very clear
and very interesting.

00:22:27.120 --> 00:22:32.120
And it's like you have a literary flare,

00:22:32.580 --> 00:22:36.750
but totally in film. (laughs)

00:22:36.750 --> 00:22:38.370
- So there were lots of iterations.

00:22:38.370 --> 00:22:40.800
There were lots of drafts,
different storylines

00:22:40.800 --> 00:22:42.690
that were completely different than this.

00:22:42.690 --> 00:22:45.960
And I even ended up doing
a pretty large rewrite

00:22:45.960 --> 00:22:47.853
four months before we filmed.

00:22:48.960 --> 00:22:50.700
You just have to sort of test it out,

00:22:50.700 --> 00:22:53.400
and a lot of the, you look at, you know,

00:22:53.400 --> 00:22:55.470
you study other movies,
you study other scripts.

00:22:55.470 --> 00:22:59.583
You see what works in
similar genres or styles.

00:23:00.870 --> 00:23:04.410
So a lot of it is sort
of copying the masters

00:23:04.410 --> 00:23:06.900
to a certain extent and just
putting it into new places

00:23:06.900 --> 00:23:09.810
and testing things out
and having good readers,

00:23:09.810 --> 00:23:12.810
people that are
screenwriters and filmmakers.

00:23:12.810 --> 00:23:15.060
And honestly, often the
best notes come from actors

00:23:15.060 --> 00:23:17.673
because they're so attuned to character.

00:23:18.870 --> 00:23:20.940
So you just wanna be open to feedback

00:23:20.940 --> 00:23:24.600
and be ready to kill your darlings

00:23:24.600 --> 00:23:27.963
because you just have to
to make the script work.

00:23:28.830 --> 00:23:29.663
- [Priya] Yep.

00:23:30.660 --> 00:23:31.770
- [Audience Member] Just a brief question,

00:23:31.770 --> 00:23:33.480
I was very impressed by the accuracy

00:23:33.480 --> 00:23:35.910
and the detail of the geology facts.

00:23:35.910 --> 00:23:37.560
Any response from the geology community

00:23:37.560 --> 00:23:39.990
about these discoveries and things, or?

00:23:39.990 --> 00:23:42.180
- The geologists are mostly very,

00:23:42.180 --> 00:23:44.230
enjoying it very much, which is exciting.

00:23:45.570 --> 00:23:48.060
Recently on Twitter, I saw
someone post a checklist

00:23:48.060 --> 00:23:49.650
of all the geology activities

00:23:49.650 --> 00:23:52.080
that they got to see in the film.

00:23:52.080 --> 00:23:53.214
So they're enjoying it.

00:23:53.214 --> 00:23:54.047
- So they approved.

00:23:54.047 --> 00:23:56.093
- They approved, they
approved for the most part.

00:23:59.700 --> 00:24:01.984
And they're also, they're really,

00:24:01.984 --> 00:24:03.188
they're interested in the story

00:24:03.188 --> 00:24:05.220
about sort of plagiarism
and bullying in academia.

00:24:05.220 --> 00:24:08.520
They're, people are, it's not the,

00:24:08.520 --> 00:24:09.990
that's not a reaction I was expecting,

00:24:09.990 --> 00:24:11.700
but people are really interested in that

00:24:11.700 --> 00:24:13.440
and wanna talk about
that because it's things

00:24:13.440 --> 00:24:15.120
that people have experienced.

00:24:15.120 --> 00:24:16.970
So there's a lot of interest in that.

00:24:18.600 --> 00:24:21.180
- I think we have time
for one more question,

00:24:21.180 --> 00:24:25.233
because they expect us to
get out of the room by six.

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:33.360
- [Audience Member] Hi, so
first, thank you for the film.

00:24:33.360 --> 00:24:36.603
I have zero academic cinema training,

00:24:37.800 --> 00:24:40.140
but I'm a big Western fan.

00:24:40.140 --> 00:24:43.140
And I guess the analogy here is not lost.

00:24:43.140 --> 00:24:47.520
It's the setting of the saving
the family ranch plot line.

00:24:47.520 --> 00:24:51.333
I guess Alex is trying to
save her academic range.

00:24:52.410 --> 00:24:57.065
I was wondering how you sort of tie

00:24:57.065 --> 00:25:01.080
a film like this together
where usually one expects

00:25:01.080 --> 00:25:02.940
some conflict between a, you know,

00:25:02.940 --> 00:25:06.300
protagonist and the villain,
and here the conflict,

00:25:06.300 --> 00:25:10.020
at least to me and
especially for an academic

00:25:10.020 --> 00:25:11.730
trying to do science, the
conflict is not really

00:25:11.730 --> 00:25:16.590
against anybody except minor
plagiarism in the beginning.

00:25:16.590 --> 00:25:21.590
There isn't really any bad
person to contrast against with.

00:25:24.210 --> 00:25:27.000
So I was sort of interested
in how that's sort of used

00:25:27.000 --> 00:25:32.000
to generate conflict against,
well, Mother Nature or-

00:25:32.850 --> 00:25:34.623
- Yeah, I think that's part of it.

00:25:34.623 --> 00:25:39.623
And you know, it's a movie in
part about the climate crisis

00:25:39.690 --> 00:25:42.840
and about extinction
events, both big and small,

00:25:42.840 --> 00:25:44.760
very old and somewhat new.

00:25:44.760 --> 00:25:49.760
So I think that sort of provides
some of the context there

00:25:50.100 --> 00:25:53.640
for the conflict and for
Eddie's character, Jason Lowrie,

00:25:53.640 --> 00:25:58.640
to also provide that sort
of push to the ranchers,

00:25:58.950 --> 00:26:02.580
which was very difficult to
do because a lot, you know,

00:26:02.580 --> 00:26:04.703
a lot of the dialogue, I didn't,

00:26:04.703 --> 00:26:07.350
you know, you hear, you would talk to BLM,

00:26:07.350 --> 00:26:09.330
Bureau of Land Management range cons,

00:26:09.330 --> 00:26:10.800
or you'd talk to ranchers,

00:26:10.800 --> 00:26:13.290
and they were often
talking past each other

00:26:13.290 --> 00:26:16.590
but trying to help each other
in certain ways as well.

00:26:16.590 --> 00:26:19.110
And so there was a natural conflict there.

00:26:19.110 --> 00:26:21.360
But I really didn't
want to make anyone out,

00:26:21.360 --> 00:26:23.850
it's more complex than good and bad,

00:26:23.850 --> 00:26:26.520
and I wanted to try to
share that complexity

00:26:26.520 --> 00:26:27.571
with some of that conflict.

00:26:27.571 --> 00:26:29.910
- Yeah, I mean, and I
think, actually I thought

00:26:29.910 --> 00:26:34.530
you succeeded beautifully
because there was this tension

00:26:34.530 --> 00:26:37.050
without an illusion to black and white.

00:26:37.050 --> 00:26:39.630
And I think I caught the backdrop.

00:26:39.630 --> 00:26:41.790
I mean, I like the extinction,

00:26:41.790 --> 00:26:44.100
the ancient extinction event

00:26:44.100 --> 00:26:47.340
and the contrast with
the current struggle.

00:26:47.340 --> 00:26:51.210
And the way that you showed,
you know, the BLM character,

00:26:51.210 --> 00:26:52.830
at the end of the day,

00:26:52.830 --> 00:26:57.830
we are all in this potential
extinction event together

00:26:58.410 --> 00:27:00.690
and a sort of a certain kind of truth.

00:27:00.690 --> 00:27:02.040
I actually liked that tension.

00:27:02.040 --> 00:27:04.410
I liked the fact that you started off

00:27:04.410 --> 00:27:06.030
with that plagiarism thing,

00:27:06.030 --> 00:27:07.800
and then that, you were done with it,

00:27:07.800 --> 00:27:09.450
you know, that sort of went away.

00:27:09.450 --> 00:27:12.510
But that riff was through there
throughout the film, right?

00:27:12.510 --> 00:27:15.090
I mean, that sort of carried on.

00:27:15.090 --> 00:27:16.740
So I actually liked the fact,

00:27:16.740 --> 00:27:20.670
and I sensed sort of tension and conflict,

00:27:20.670 --> 00:27:22.440
not so much between good and bad

00:27:22.440 --> 00:27:26.670
but between almost like
thriving and survival,

00:27:26.670 --> 00:27:27.810
thriving and survival, you know,

00:27:27.810 --> 00:27:29.640
academic, thriving, what it takes,

00:27:29.640 --> 00:27:32.100
and then it's like down to survival.

00:27:32.100 --> 00:27:34.470
And you know, I thought
that those tensions

00:27:34.470 --> 00:27:37.140
were really, really well done, so.

00:27:37.140 --> 00:27:38.290
- Thank you.
- It was,

00:27:39.270 --> 00:27:42.630
I mean, I'm stunned that, you
know, I was going to ask you,

00:27:42.630 --> 00:27:44.820
you've not been in a PhD program, right?

00:27:44.820 --> 00:27:45.960
- No. (laughs)

00:27:45.960 --> 00:27:47.117
- Yeah, so I was pretty stunned

00:27:47.117 --> 00:27:51.990
at the sort of the layers
that you managed to get at.

00:27:51.990 --> 00:27:55.410
So if there are no further questions,

00:27:55.410 --> 00:27:58.013
let's all thank Jonathan
again for a fantastic film.

00:27:58.013 --> 00:28:00.647
- Thank you, Priya.
(audience applauding)

00:28:00.647 --> 00:28:02.428
- And to many, many more.

00:28:02.428 --> 00:28:03.695
- Thank you.
- Yes, yes.

00:28:03.695 --> 00:28:05.160
And thank you so much for coming today.

00:28:05.160 --> 00:28:06.310
- Of course, of course.

